            <ArticleTitle>Prediction by computerised tomography of distance from skin to epidural space during thoracic epidural insertion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>701-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this single group observational study on 29 patients, we describe a technique that predicts the depth of the epidural space, calculated from the routine pre-operative chest computerised tomography (CT) scan using Pythagorean triangle trigonometry. We also compared the CT-derived depth of the epidural space with the actual depth of needle insertion. The CT-derived and the actual depths of the epidural space were highly correlated (r = 0.88, R2 = 0.78, p &lt; 0.0001). The mean (95% CI) difference between CT-derived and actual depths was 0.26 (0.03-0.49) cm. Thus, the CT-derived depth tends to be greater than the actual depth by between 0.03 and 0.49 cm. There were no associations between either the CT-derived or the actual depth of the epidural space and age, weight, height or body mass index.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
